## 1. Introduction
## 1. Introduction
The rapid development of the integrated electronic technologies and portable devices, such as wearable devices, tablets, mobile phones, laptops, and increased oil prices, air pollution, political tensions, lots of companies and institutes showed great demands to introduce new reliable, lightweight, intelligent, highperformance, sustainable, eco-friendly and flexible energy storage devices. Among many promising electrochemical devices, batteries and supercapacitors have been extensively used as outstanding energy storage devices in various applications. Since both batteries and supercapacitors have globally attracted lots of satisfactions, tremendous efforts have been widely sacrificed to improve their efficiencies where employed in different applications [1–7]. To meet the highest productivity, a large number of researchers devoted immense efforts to develop new strategies and techniques to enhance the electrochemical performance. The renewable power sources due to their current limitations are still in developing steps and it needs more investigations to employ them in all aspects of our life. More reliable, safe, flexible, and wearable properties are in direct agreement with our lifestyle, economic growth and energy suppliers, which needs more attentions and assessments [8–15]. Originally, the universal consumers have preferred to use fossil fuels while our globe requires to devote more focusing on clean sources and renewable energies. Despite from the high demands for power sources in the future civilizations, it is obvious, renewable energies and real demands for storing the originated electricity has arisen enormously. As a most considered energy storage, the global supercapacitor market has been experiencing a steady progress over the last decade. And it is predicted they can acquire more share of market in near future [16–17]. The extensive research on energy storage devices derived from their drawbacks which limits their applications in large-scale and more industrial subjects. Compared to the batteries, with high energy density, the employment of supercapacitors limited by low values of energy density while provide high power density, long cycle life, and promising durability. Then, many efforts have been carried out to provide desired flexibility and aforementioned features [18–23].
## 1.1. Supercapacitors: EDLC and pseudocapacitance
Up to now, different materials have been widely used for developing energy storage and energy conversion systems to overcome their problems [13,24–31]. Among different energy storage devices, supercapacitors (SCs) known as promising electrical storage that enjoy from fast recharging with a large amount of released power. They are basically outlined to overpass the gap between capacitors and batteries to present high specific energy as well as fast charging. Fig. 1, shows a schematic of the electron position and typical process of charging, fully charged, and discharging in supercapacitors. The results of recent research have been a diligent attempt to cover up battery failures that have so far hampered their use. The main problem with batteries is that they do not deliver the required power density when it necessary. Concerning the clean, efficient, affordable, safe, and replacing fossil energy, grid-scale energy storage systems are urgently necessary, where Na-ion, Li-ion, Zn-ion and metal-air batteries could supply crucial support, due to abundant materials raw and an excellent electrochemical mechanism. Consequently, an extensive attempts have been widely sacrificed for advanced anode and cathode materials with exceptional electrochemical performance is in crucial challenge for next-generation rechargeable batteries [33–40]. Compared to the typical batteries, supercapacitors due to low energy densities, are in critical challenge to use efficiently in different applications. Fig. 2 schematically compares the high energy density and power density in batteries and SCs. it means in a typical run of supercapacitor, a massive amount of power is delivered to the electrical devices that can be pleasured by many users, while batteries with a higher amount of energy density take more minutes to charge an electrical device. Generally, SCs called as
## Supercapacitor Classification
ultra-capacitor or electric double-layer capacitor (EDLC) in case of excellent capacitance rather than a regular capacitor. And also, by providing the rapid charge and discharge process, they show fast acceleration delivery and much improved regenerative braking energy. Typically, a supercapacitor device constructed from a well-designed electrode (large surface, conducive, well-pore sized and distributed) and an organic or aqueous electrolyte which have a vital role in electron pathways. To preventing short circuit between two designed electrodes, the third part of a SC is a separator [42–46].
Concerning of commercially applicable energy storage devices, fast charging, stability and longevity with extensive usage are recommended options to achieve high electrochemical performance. Actually, supercapacitors, which provide excellent power handling capabilities with implementation of fast charge rates, are incorporated are in agreement of utilizing high porous and conductive materials and a number of researchers are responsible to consider the possibility and feasibility of using Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), graphene, CNTs or nanomaterials as efficient electrode toward supercapacitors [48–56]. To store charges, supercapacitors operate according to two well-known procedures of electrical double layer capacitor (EDLC) and Pseudocapacitance as shown in Fig. 3. In EDLC, the capacitive procedure relies on the electrostatic interactions (adsorption) between the conductive electrode surface and electrolytic ions. Through applying a voltage, two layers of negative and positive ions are formed (at electrode surface, and in the electrolyte) and then, the two formed layers of ions are separated by a single layer of solvent which adheres to the electrode surface and operates as a di-electric in a typical capacitor. While in pseudocapacitors the overall capacitance originated from faradaic reactions containing reduction–oxidation reactions (redox reactions), Electro-sorption, and intercalation processes through an electron charge transfer between electrolyte electrode [58–66]. Basically, the employed electrode materials have a main role in presented storing mechanism. Recently, supercapacitors have been widely employed in many research areas due to unique properties and thus, the widespread acceptance of ultracapacitors has led to the introducing several methods to enhance their performance including constructing hybrid systems, modification of surface electrodes, and new designed/synthesized electrode materials (Fig. 4). To improve the electrochemical feature of supercapacitors, various class of carbon-based materials such as graphene, carbon nanotubes, activated carbon, carbon aerogel and carbon black have been terrifically used in supercapacitors [68–76]. Besides, the new type of metal organic frameworks with ability of structural tailoring, high surface area, and permeability to guest molecules, have attracted enormous attentions to use in supercapacitors. In this review, we are going to consider the filed patents over MOF-employment in supercapacitor devices as well as challenging the role of metal organic frameworks, their composites and derivatives where used as an electrode material in supercapacitors with improved efficiency. One of the critical challenges over the metal organic frameworks, is their conductivity. Basically, metal organic frameworks, present very low conductivity being not favored of electrochemical devices. Nevertheless, metal organic frameworks when combined with conducting materials such as conductive polymers, graphene or metals, present high potential to employ as leading electrodes in SC devices.
## 1.2. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs)
Since the emergence of super-porous Metal-Organic Framework (MOFs) materials which are also introduced as porous coordination polymers (PCPs) in the middle 90 s by intensive research of Professor Yaghi and his co-workers, there has been a tremendous interest in the field of applied nanomaterials in chemistry, chemical engineering, material science, and related areas [79–87]. MOFs are currently introduced as highly crystalline, sponge-like materials composed of two components: organic molecules called as linkers and metal ions [89–91]. Based on the released resources from CCDC institute1 the variety of existing linkers and metal ions applicable to MOF synthesis has allowed the unlimited development of these types of materials in one- dimension (1-D) [86,91], two- dimension (2-D), and three-dimension (3-D) for the targeted applications so that more than 96,000 MOFs has been deposited in Crystal Cambridge Data Center (CCDC) so far . The properties structures of MOFs can be suitably tuned for a specific application depending on the linker and metal units. Due to this distinctive hallmark of MOF materials as well as their facile synthesis procedures and excellent porosity, and also other unique characteristics of MOFs consisting significant large surface-areas, structural flexibility, and great mechanical and thermal stabilities, they have found aroused potential applications in different disciplines including drug delivery, medical imaging, pharmaceuticals, catalysis and photo-catalysis, gas adsorption and separation, sensors, waste-water treatment, batteries and more recently supercapacitors [93–99]. The latter application of MOFs especially in Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) and supercapacitors or generally electrochemical systems, has attracted intensive attention in recent years owing to some unique characteristics of MOFs such as stable and permanent porosity, great surface area, and ability to control of pore shapes as well as dimension. The high surface area and permanent pores of MOFs allow us to prepare electrodes with significant high storage capacity for Li-ions. Moreover, existing metal clusters inside MOF structures make them as promising electrode materials containing inherent redox active sites (redox reaction between the electrode and the electrolyte) [59]. Compared to inorganic conventional materials, some exceptionally inherent hallmarks of MOFs involving high porosity, abundant pore, multiple functional ligands, great surface, electrochemically active metal centers, easily tunable pore structures, and post-synthetic modification using organic functional groups have generated tremendous amount of interest for applications in various disciplines such as batteries and supercapacitors. Nonetheless, the practical application of MOFs in these areas has been limited by some important shortcomings including low conductivity, unsatisfied chemical stability, and usually high fabrication expenses. To resolve these limitations of pristine MOFs, producing metal oxides or carbonaceous materials from MOFs have been promising candidates [101–101]. More impressively, in addition to the direct and solitary use of MOFs, they can also be employed as composite with different types of materials such as carbons family (e.g. graphene, carbon nanotubes, carbon fiber, carbon aerogel and activated carbon), metal nanoparticles, metaloxides, polyoxometalates, and polymers in all mentioned applications especially LIBs and supercapacitors. These composite materials resolve low conductivity intrinsic problem of pristine MOFs which prevents to attain an acceptable levels of charge transfer in LIBs and supercapacitors. Nevertheless, the introduction of abovementioned materials to MOFs should not majorly affect their outstanding properties of high surface area and porosity [103–105].
On the other hand, MOFs can be used as ideal templates to prepare other porous nanostructures such as metal oxides and carbon materials with significant high surface area. In all the above pure or composite applications of MOFs, the correct synthesis of materials is of particular importance so that inadequate synthesis can lead to undesirable properties. Thus, because of the importance of precise and accurate MOF synthesis and its vital role in structural properties, firstly, in the next section we will have a concise review on synthesis techniques used for pure and composite MOF materials. Many applications of MOFs have been investigated globally for above points. Here we are going to review the important and new applications of this material in supercapacitors based on the patents as a major source of evaluating the employability of MOF materials in the energy storage field with statistical and technical aspects of the claims. Although studies and developments in this field are not as extensive as other areas but we believe that this application of MOFs will be studied widely and this review is a new window to encourage researchers to investigate about this area to develop an application of MOFs for improving supercapacitors performance. Moreover, many strategies and developments in synthesizing composite MOF materials are reviewed to improve the MOF properties to apply in traditional LIBs and supercapacitors, especially, to solve (resolve) main disadvantage of low electrical conductivity. By reviewing of researches, it is crystal clear that pioneering of MOFs with larger surface and high electrical conductivity is a critical issue to enable them for energy storage applications [107–114].
One of the most significant limitations of MOFs which influences their practical applications is their poor stability. Different aspects of MOF stability including thermal, mechanical, and chemical can be considered where the chemical one refers to their resistance when they are exposed to various chemicals. The poor chemical stability is attributed to the nature of bonding in MOF structures where unlike covalent bonds of many materials, MOFs are formed through reversible coordination bonds between metal ions and organic linkers. The chemical stability of MOFs can be tuned using different efficient methods for both new MOFs (via de novo synthesis) and existing MOFs (via improving their stability). For example, one of the most efficient methods to synthesize new stable MOFs, is employing high-valent metals such as ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , $A|^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , to bond with oxygen-containing donor ligands forming strong MOF structures with high chemical stability in different environments like moisture, acid, and base. On the other hand for existing MOFs, several strategies have been developed where the post-synthetic exchange (refers to metal center or ligand replacement) and post-synthetic ligand modification are known promising tools to improve the chemical stability of MOFs [116–116].
## 1.3. Synthetic methods for metal–organic frameworks
## 1.3.1. Pristine MOFs
As mentioned earlier, the use of pristine MOF materials as electrodes for supercapacitor has been the subject of much attention due to their great surface area and intrinsic redox properties. In this context, many metal ions such as Ni, Fe, Co, Mn, Cu, Zn, Ce, V, Cr, Al, Zr, and organic linkers like big families of Terephthalic acids, imidazolates, and carboxylic acids, various types of bipyridine, benzoic acids, and phenylene have been employed in the production of pure MOFs for such application. Worthwhile mentioning, that most of these kinds of ligands show weak electron withdrawing property which leads to act as a redox center. Redox reaction specially in ligands containing oxygen like carboxylic acids is occurred through oxygen groups plying a vital role for Li insertion/deinsertion or delocalization of charge along the MOF [100,117–121]. In addition, almost all ligands have one or more aromatic ring in their structures which can be occupied by Li ions [118–119,123]. Fig. 5 illustrates the probable lithium sites around oxygen atoms and above aromatic rings [119]. Simulation results of Maiti et al., has demonstrated that each aromatic ring can attract six $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion [118]. Therefore, higher capacity is expected for large ligands due to two reasons, i) they create larger pore volume and surface area ii) they have more rings to absorb more $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion.
On the other hand, some 2-dimentional (2-D) pristine MOFs such as $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(2,3,6,7,10,11$ -hexaiminotriphenylene) $)_{2}$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2})$ and hexaaminobenzene (HAB)-derived MOF have been synthesized with high porosity and surface area as well as great electrical conductivity [124–124]. It is believed that the high conductivity in 2-D MOFs is originated from electronic communication of metal centers and in-plane charge delocalization [60,125].
Zirconium due to its easy availability, low toxicity, and the high oxidation stat (Zr (IV)) has been a favor metal ion for construction Zr-based MOF materials. Because of strong affinity between Zr (IV) atoms and oxygen atoms of carboxylate ligands, these types of MOF structures exhibit outstanding stability tolerating organic solvents and water, and even acidic media and have found potential applications in many practical disciplines. The strong nature of coordination bonds of Zi (IV) ions and carboxylate linkers refers to their acidity and basicity characteristics where they are known as hard acid and base respectively [116,126–129]. Although most of the famous and well-known Zr-based MOFs like UIO-66 UIO-67,UIO-68, MOF-808, and MIL-153 are dominated by 3-D structures, 2-D Zr-based MOFs have gained many attentions after introducing DUT-84 [127]. DUT-84 is known as the first framework from Zr-based MOF family with a 2-D topology. In addition to high porosity and surface area, these types of 2-D Zr-based MOFs, provide some interesting hallmark including easy accessibility to metal sites and functional groups, enhanced chemical activity, optical transparency, and mechanical flexibility (due to their thin layered structures). The presence of these properties especially the latter one can lead to producing flexible materials with application in batteries and supercapacitors. In addition to DUT-84 which was firstly introduced by Bon et al. [127], UMCM-309a by Ma research group [128] and recently, UCY-13 and UCY-14 by Tasiopoulos et al. [126] have been developed for application in various fields. As mentioned earlier, the synthesis of MOF structures is particular of significance due to its direct influences on MOF properties and subsequently their applications in a number of related fields. MOFs are generally synthesized by incorporating metal salts and organic ligands in Polar and usually high boiling solvents like di-alkyl formamides, acetonitrile or even deionized water according to solvothermal reactions at medium temperatures approximately lower than $300~^{0}\mathsf{C}.$ The required energy for taking place of the chemical reaction between metal salts and organic ligands is majorly supplied by conventional heating in an oven. However, other sources of heating, i.e., microwave, electromagnetic radiation, ultrasonic waves, and mechanical grinding can be alternatively employed for MOFs synthesis. Fig. 6 illustrates a simple overview of different synthesis methods and their energy sources.
Regardless the type of energy source, several parameters such as the concentrations of metal salt and the concentrations of a ligand with large and different range, pH, reaction duration and the solubility can influence the product formed and its morphology which is important to achieve suitable MOFs with desirable electrochemical performance for supercapacitor applications.
## 1.3.2. MOF composites
Low-conductivity of the most pristine MOFs causes a number of efforts to synthesize new composite MOFs with desirable properties. Generally, MOF structures can be modified through their synthesis named as in-situ synthesis or after that via post-synthetic procedures. In both in-situ and post-synthetic routes, a MOF with desirable electrochemical properties can be produced.
## 1.3.3. MOF/carbon composite
Different types of carbonaceous materials, including activated carbon, carbon nanotubes (single walled and multiwalled), graghene, graphene oxide (GO), reduced graphene oxide (RGO), carbon aerogels and hydrogels, and carbon fibers have been employed for improving electrical conductivity and mechanostability properties in MOF structures so far [60,130–131]. In the most of works, the combination of MOF structures with carbon-based materials is performed through in-situ synthesis using hydrothermal or room temperature methodology. In this synthesis approach that can be performed through one-pot or stepwise procedures, MOFs could be formed on the carbonaceous scaffolds or be encapsulated into carbon matrix. Fig. 7 typically demonstrates a convenient rout for one-pot in-situ synthesis of a MOF/carbon composite [133–134]. As it can be observed, under hydrothermal reaction carbonaceous materials are added into a solution containing metal ions and organic linkers as precursors of MOFs.
## 1.3.4. MOF/ metal or meal oxide nanoparticles
Integrating metal and metal oxide nanoparticles with MOF structures strengthen the pseudocapacitance property of MOFs because of increasing of redox centers [60,136–137]. This composite can be prepared through four routes as illustrating in Fig. 8 [138]. First method is based on infiltrating the precursors of nanoparticles within the prepared MOF and formation of nanoparticles by application of heat (a). In the second method which is known as ‘‘one-pot” approach, MOF crystals are formed in the presence of pre-formed metal or metal oxide nanoparticles (b). In another approach, a core–shell inorganic particles containing inorganic nodes of MOF as shell and metal or metal oxide nanoparticle as core are used. In this method inorganic nodes are released from
