## 1.3.5. MOF/POM composite
Similar to the metal and metal oxide nanoparticles, redox centers of MOF materials can be increased through combination of MOF structures with polyoxometalates (POMs) [102,139–141].
Generally, MOF/POM composites can be divided in two main groups of POM-based MOF and POM-loaded MOF materials. In the first group, POMs can be part of frameworks while in the latter set MOFs are loaded around POM centers [143–145].
Generally, the preparation of POM-based MOF materials is performed by adding POM precursor into a solution of metal ions and organic linkers through conventional heating methods like hydroor solvo-thermal. On the other hand, POM-loaded MOF materials are synthesized through three methods similar to what described for MOF/metal oxide nanoparticles composite. Briefly, POMloaded MOF materials can be prepared via (i) formation of MOF network in the presence of POMs, (ii) synthesis of POM clusters inside the pre-formed MOF network, and iii) combination of preformed MOF and POM in a solution [148–149].
## 1.3.6. MOF/polymers
Incorporating of MOF materials with conducting polymers such as polyaniline (PANI), polypyrrole (Ppy), and polyethylene dioxythiophene (PEDOT) to achieve a MOF/polymer composite is another strategy to reduce low conductivity shortcoming of MOF materials. Generally, three types of polymer@MOF, MOF@polymer and polyMOF composites are synthesized and have been attracting much attention in many fields of application [151–153]. The composite could be achieved by growth of MOFs on the polymer surfaces through in-situ approach or by polymer inclusion inside MOF networks [152]. On the other hand, coating MOFs with a conductive polymer layer via chemical polymerization method will result in MOF@polymer composite [152,154]. 2, 6-lutidine in this approach, an end group of polymer molecule is binding to the vacant metal coordination sites on the surface of MOFs. Moreover, in polyMOF composites, an amorphous polymer is utilized as organic ligand to form MOF-polymer hybrid materials [155]. Fig. 10 shows different synthetic procedures described for polymer/MOF composite materials.
## 1.3.7. Mixed-metals MOFs
As the name of mixed metals MOFs indicates, these types of metal organic frameworks comprise at least two different kinds of metal ions as inorganic center in their structures. Preparation of these types of MOF materials is usually carried out through convenient one-pot approach where a solution containing of desired metals is added to linker solution under moderate heating condition. Alternatively, in a relatively difficult post-synthetic method, some metal ions of monometallic MOF are exchanged with different and compatible metal ions through soaking in a concentrated solution of the desired metal [86,158–159]. Fig. 11 typically illustrates differences between methods utilized in synthesis of mixed metals MOF materials.
## 1.3.8. MOF-derived carbons and metal oxides
Porous Carboneous materials known as the most widely used component in fabrication of electrodes in supercapacitors owing to their good stability, great conductivity and relatively high surface area. The latter property could be also impressively improved by the choosing special precursors like MOF during their synthesis. Therefore, fabrication of MOF-derived carbons can be directly obtained by pyrolysis of MOFs at high temperature under an inert atmosphere, followed by an acid treatment (Fig. 12). The presence of organic linkers inside MOF structures provides a good carbon source to synthesize the MOF-derived carbons. More interestingly, the resulting carbons can show different textural properties based on what kind of MOFs have been selected as precursors [161–162].
On the other hand, similar to carbons, due to favorable psedocapacitive properties of metal oxides they have attracted many attentions in fabrication of materials as electrodes for supercapacitors. Nonetheless, metal oxides suffer from very low specific surface area which makes diffusion of ions difficult through electrodes. Therefore, metal oxides with large surface area prepared from MOFs as precursors or templates to achieve MOFderived metal oxides are found to be favorable alternative for fabrication of materials for electrodes. These types of metal oxides are generally fabricated through most-common procedure of calcination (annealing) so that the MOFs as source are heated in a furnace in air atmosphere at a few hundred degree [165–166]. Fig. 13 illustrates a schematic of magnified scheme of ZIF-67 comprising Pd nano particles (NPs) encapsulated Co ions–organic linker composite and SEM images of PdO loaded $\mathsf{p}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ HNCs (hollow polyhedron nanocubes) with obvious hollow polyhedral structures with threefold edges. Worthwhile mentioning, MOF-derived mixed metal oxides can be readily obtained when a mixed metal MOF is used as precursor. More importantly, MOF-derived carbon/ metal oxide composites can be produced through sequential thermal treating of MOFs under inert and air atmospheres [167].
## 2. MOF applications in supercapacitors
The prominent features of MOFs, along with their porosity and crystallinity and the possibility for tailoring both their properties and structure on demand, have attracted the concern of researchers owing to their tremendous diversity of engrossing structural topologies and great range applications as functional materials for several applications, including catalysis, ion exchange, chirality, biomedicine, optics, adsorption, energy storage, magnetism and electronics, sensing, gas separation and storage.
Metal-organic frameworks propose valuable new catalytic chemistry, already having been exposed to catalyze an allinclusive class of reactions including many of industrial interest. As the reaction requirements, such as the type of the reactants and product and the temperature, are crucial to verifying the viability of MOFs as effective catalysts, it is indisputable that not only their selectivities and activities but also their stabilities are essential. The impacts of reactants and products, such as oxygen and oxygen-containing materials, have not been specifically defined in any depth, and they are required for improving the stabilities of MOFs as catalysts. During the reactions requiring strong acidity and those involving profoundly reactive metal kinds, and reactions of unsaturated hydrocarbons, MOF catalysts can be deactivated cause of Coking as a prevailing intricacy for all of the hydrocarbon conversion reactions. Besides, coking-based deactivation will be lethal, because MOFs cannot be restored at high temperatures. Utilization of MOFs as catalysts are anticipated to be limited to low-temperature purposes, to hydrocarbons rather than oxygencontaining compounds and reductive rather than oxidative conditions [170–170]. Furthermore, chiral MOFs have presented grand insurance as extremely practical and recyclable heterogeneous catalysts in asymmetric reactions. According to the recent assessments and studies of basic theories and potential merits of MOFs in catalysis, the following critical concerns attributes to the original perception of MOF asymmetric catalysis are considered as substrate diffusion through the MOF channels, approaches to erect and provide reagent-accessible channels, the dependence of catalytic activities, framework distortion and determining of large unrestricted channels in MOFs, and stereoselectivities on MOF open channel sizes, and specific illustration of structure–property links by single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformations MOFs also implement absolute principles to combine versatile functionalities in a structurally organized fashion. MOFs containing different catalytic markets can be formed and applied to catalyze a list of reactions in a tandem method, heading to atom-economic chemical transformations. Cooperative catalysis can additionally be imagined with MOFs by owning two catalytic centers operating together to synergistically activate the organic substrates. MOF structures can conceivably work as electron shuttles or excitedstate channels to promote redox- and photo-driven asymmetric reactions. These porous materials have a splendid fate in heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis, and a new field of chirotechnology can be assumed with this emerging class of hybrid materials [172– 172]. Concerning the active site architecture, reasonable command over the skeleton flexibility of the material may further hold a large impact on its definitive catalytic properties. Although framework flexibility has been considerably disregarded when outlining catalytic applications of MOFs, it can be supposed that this attribute will be weighed in future developments. Exerting enzymes as a source of inspiration, the aspiration has to be the preparation of materials capable of readjusting their pore space by conformational changes of their building units [173].
The rational design and controlled synthesis of multifunctional MOF-based compounds for different purposes, such as controllable drug release, optics, imaging, and biosensing, are welcome for the embodiment and quantification of therapeutics and clinical diagnostics. Generally, the utilization of MOFs, their derivatives in the electrochemical sensing area is still at an early stage, and there is huge scope for innovative investigation in this thrilling field. With the joint forces of authorities from multidisciplinary fields, one could anticipate that many breakthroughs will be obtained shortly [174]. Alongside the structural properties, numerous features of MOF based on electrochemical, electroluminescent, and optical principles should be explored more comprehensively to extend chances to increase other applications. To address prospective inquiries in the sensing field, it is crucial to overcome the current shortcomings in the current technology, including reproducibility, selectivity, stability, durability, response-time, and sensitivity. It is firmly agreed that lots of weights should be placed to promote and engage real-world sensors to assist enhance the practicability of MOFs [176–178].
MOF-based functional materials have been extensively appreciated for their distinguished accomplishment in numerous advanced energy applications, including their functions as bifunctional cathode catalysts for oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in metal-air batteries. MOFbased compounds have been placed high assumptions on replacing the more precious but less preserved expensive metal-based catalysts. The worth of MOFs arises from their versatile coordinating frameworks, tunable chemical compositions, and crystalline porous structures. Their derivatives can partly inherit the abovementioned benefits with even upgraded chemical and structural attributes. Despite the satisfying development with MOF-based catalysts for metal-air batteries, at the current platform of experimentation, most activities rely on the non-discriminative strategies to grow overall performance in both OER and ORR. Forthcoming research on MOF-based materials for metal-air batteries may further discover the approaches to realize bifunctional activities in MOF-containing catalysts through discriminative mechanisms or catalytic units, which positively meet the ‘‘uniqueness” of MOFs over other functional materials. The current experience and understanding will not only profit the framework and synthesis of MOF-containing catalysts for metal-air batteries but can additionally be lengthened to other energy appeals that need bifunctional catalytic activities, (e.g. water splitting), and metalion batteries [180–180].
The excellent synergistic combination features of MOFs and other materials made MOF composites to be acknowledged as highly superior porous materials, While, the reported research on the structural changes of the MOFs, electrode modification, and electrode surface during the charging/discharging is still in the beginning steps. Moreover, the bonding mechanism between the MOF and the combined material or the distribution of particles in the hybrid structure has a little exposed data and needs to be more discovered. Besides, the structure and morphology of the electrode material is a challenging subject towards the insertion of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ , as profound research is required to be carried out on the controlling morphology during the synthesis process [181]. Typically, most of the introduced synthesis procedure for MOF compounds carry out at high temperatures without having fine sensibilities control of structure and morphology. Therefore, such research will address their synthesis more reproducible, which in turn will benefit promote the practical commercialization of the output or project. Insulating nature of the MOFs is another obstacle that address more investigations to modify their structure through effective synthesis strategies based on their physical properties, including electronic conductivity and charge transfer mechanisms it can be clarified that the modified graphene (an allotrope of graphite), possesses a much higher conductivity than graphite. By cutting down the expenses of the MOF producing at Market-friendly prices, these materials can be more involved with energy storage devices mainly batteries. This object can be achieved by providing more affordable and abundant components extracted easily in nature or replacing hybrid solvents with expensive organic solutions. Porous architecture of MOFs not only affords an interesting occasion to act as both separator and electrode to get rid of the additional usage of extra separator layer material but also provides a chance to be exploited to dope redox-active species which can improve the electrochemical performance. Meanwhile, a large surface to volume ratios enables them to invent low-weight batteries which will indirectly drive to a higher specific capacity. Computational techniques are regularly a requisite option to predesign the best possible electrode/electrolyte–electrode/current collector system, which is challenging in accomplishing high cycle stability of lithium-ion batteries (LIBs). Besides, the active material wasting can be optimized by thickness control of material coating that prevents the extra costs and weight of the overall device. However, the conventional methods such as drop-casting, dip coating, and spin coating are in the technological challenge for the optimal and homogenous deposition of MOF layers on the electrode surface, they cannot provide essential control over layer thickness. Therefore, developing high-temperature vacuum press or inkjet printing approaches are in progress to get desirable results. The type of the linker and doping appropriate electroactive species is a potential area of future research in order to enhance the electrochemical activity and deliver much-desired advances in the capacity and cycle stability of the next-generation LIBs. Suitable structural and morphological modifications in MOFs with such optimizations are demanding to restrain the shuttle effect and overpotential barriers encountered during charging/discharging. Bimetallic MOFs are deemed as potential candidates to compose developed LIBs as they can suggest the synthesis of electrode materials with high porosity consolidated with a high degree of graphitization. 2-D MOFs are good candidates to sustain efficient intercalation and de-intercalation of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ . The intercalation of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ in 2-D MOFs can circumvent the difficulty of the engendering of irreversible products. Therefore, 2-D MOFs with efficient interacting of metal clusters and linkers are remarkably helpful to design batteries with supreme reversibility. Furthermore, innovative heterostructures with intriguing features such as the shortage of in-situ catalysts, resolving the pure gas supplying into the system, and the suppression of shuttle effect will be accomplished through the combination of 2-D MOFs with other 2- D materials, such as metal dichalcogenides (e.g., $\mathsf{W T e}_{2}$ , $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{W S}}_{2}$ $\mathsf{M o S e}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ ). MOFs can also reduce the expenses of electrodes by combining with biowaste derived carbon. The employment of biowaste derived carbon is commonly recommended for the preparation of efficient supercapacitor electrodes and it may be equally beneficial for the growth of low-cost and efficient LIBs. As a result, pure MOFs and their derivatives and composites offer immense potential in the growth of the next-generation efficient LIBs by multiple roles of covering electrodes, purifying membranes, and catalysts. The optimization of Li sites in MOFs, exploring the dark side of the mechanism of lithium interaction with MOF, designing highly tunable and well-distributed pores, deeply comprehending of the catalytic activity, and modifying of the pore size and volume of the MOFs for hosting ionic liquids, are a couple of current and future challenges needed to assess the affordable and environmental-friendly MOF-based LIBs to overcome the prevailing practical barriers. [180,182–186].
MOFs as novel zeolite-like and porous materials are built up from rare earth or transition metal ions as nodes and multidentate organic ligands containing N- or O-donors as linkers [188–191]. Particularly, the privileged textural properties metal organic frameworks, and presenting a pore matrix with regular and fixed dimensions or geometry, proposed them as outstanding porous materials to operate as host matrices for encapsulation, or as the exclusive space where a reaction is carried out, affecting the distribution of products and reaction rate [193–195]. Among the various applications of MOFS, they presented promising output for energy storage systems such as supercapacitors, batteries, photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, sensors, and fuel cells. Besides, metal organic frameworks as support substrates have been widely employed to introduce sacrificial nanomaterials and incorporation of functional metal oxides and compounds. By beneficial of unique features of metal organic frameworks and their composites, lots of energy application fields are studied [152,196–198]. Metal organic frameworks categorized in new porous materials which have obtained many attentions during recent years with important industrial utilization. Metal organic frameworks constructed from open frames consisting of metal-centered secondary building units (SBUs) linked by organic linkers with the nature of crystalline and long-range order displaying. These incorporation forms 1-D, 2-D and 3-D networks [200–201]. Extraordinarily uniformed channels or pores manage intrinsically within the framework, and they often host guest molecules such as counter ions which balance the overall charges on the framework which originated from the charged metal nodes or solvent molecules which incorporated during the preparation method. Compared to typical porous zeolites and carbons, metal organic frameworks present a prominent ‘direct tailoring’ by selecting the favorite linker and metal [201,202–205]. The properties of metal organic frameworks strongly affected by employed metal or metal alloys sites. During the synthesis procedure, metals linked to counter ions or labile solvent molecules since they behave as Lewis acid. And then, the weak originated bonded species removed through activation of metal organic frameworks which result in the configuration of unsaturated metal centers. Typically, metal organic frameworks have coordinatively unsaturated open metal sites made into the pore ‘‘walls” in a regular and repeating mode [206] such as MIL100 (metal carboxylate) [208–211] and HKUST-1 (Cu-btc, btc $^{\circ=1,3,5}$ -benzene tricarboxylate) [213–213], indicated catalytic activity to the materials while, some metal organic frameworks show heterogeneous catalytic activity in organic synthesis [215– 217]. The electron-defective character of MOFs brings the impressive oxidation catalysts for thioethers and alcohols. Furthermore, the qualified positive charges on the metal sites can improve the properties of general adsorption which is defined as an action plan for rising Hydrogen adsorption in metal organic frameworks [219– 220].
Ultrahigh porosity, great internal surface areas, and extraordinary degree of variability in organic linkers and inorganic substances have introduced MOF materials as the most promising candidates for applications in many disciplines. Green gas storage (i.e. $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ ), selective gas adsorption and separation, drug delivery, catalysis and photocatalysis, membranes, luminescence, sensors and biosensors, magnetic properties, fuel cell, hazardous contaminants removal (e.g. heavy metals, dyes, sulfur and nitrogen-containing compounds), thin film devices, biomedical imaging, batteries, and supercapacitors are the most significant feasible applications of MOFs reported in literature so far In addition to enormous surface area of MOFs, their low density and controllable structures has made them as the most promising candidate for clean energy applications such as adsorbents for hydrogen and methane storage, membrane for hydrogen separation, electrolyte membranes and hydrogen production and storage in fuel cell systems, electrodes for rechargeable batteries and supercapacitors, and thin film devices [82,199,221–226].
One of the most pioneer reviews over MOF applications was presented by H.C. Zhou and his co-workers in 2009 [227]. Almost all aspects of MOF materials from synthesis procedures to particular applications like luminescence and drug storage were taken into account by that time. One year later a more specifically article regarding MOF application in clean energy area was revealed on $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption by S. Ma and H.C. Zhou [228]. In 2012, a comprehensive review on hydrogen storage in MOF with emphasizing on improving $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity was performed by M.P. Suh group [224]. Separation of valuable and green gases like hydrogen and methane form feed mixture using MOF membranes and MOF-based mixed matrix membranes was reviewed by H.K. Jeong and S. Keskin research groups respectively in 2012 [229] and 2013 [230]. Furthermore, a review on application of MOF as the efficient membranes for separation of valuable components from liquid and gas phases was fulfilled by S. Qiu et al. in 2014 [231]. S.L. Li and Q. Xu in 2013 [232] precisely reviewed the first
MOF applications in clean energy processes including hydrogen production and storage, fuel cells, Li-ion rechargeable batteries, supercapacitors and solar cells. Moreover, at the same year Gao et al [199] investigated the progress of MOF materials in fuel cell technologies with emphasizing on fuel generation, catalysts for cathode, electrolyte membranes, and hydrogen storage, as well as their promises and challenges. Precise review on catalysis application of MOFs in hydrogen production under light sources was also conducted by S. Pullen and S. Ott in 2016 [233] where their focus was over the pristine and composite MOF-based photocatalysts. Application of MOF in thin films from challenges in their fabrications to applications in electronic, photonic, and sensing devices as well as separation and catalysis has been reviewed by several research groups from 2012 to 2019 [151,234–237]. Two of the best of those reviews were conducted by M.D. Allendorf in 2014 [235] and D. Lu group in 2019 [237], where MOF-based electronic and opto-electronic devices and 2-D MOF membrane and thin films were investigated, respectively.
More recently, special focuses on application of MOFs in various types of batteries like lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs), and lithium-oxygen batteries (LOBs), supercapacitors and electrochemical energy storage devices have been devoted and reviewed by Q. Xu, X. Zhang and A.E. Baumann research groups, respectively [239–240]. Special review on MOF applications for LSBs [241] and LIBs [223] have been recently carried out by H. Pang and P. Cheng research groups separately.
Eventually, a comprehensive review article indicating different aspects of MOF applications in clean energy systems has been declared by $\mathbb{Q}.$ Xu et al [196]. Application of MOFs and their derivatives in $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ storage adsorbents, catalysis and photocatalysis for hydrogen production, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ photoreduction, photoelectric conversion, fuel cells, oxygen reduction reaction, batteries, supercapasitors, and water electrolysis systems have been precisely pursued in this article.
Metal-organic frameworks due to their large surface area, highly designable structures, controllable porosity, and tunable composition have widely arisen as highly engaging precursors or templates to produce different derivative of porous transition metal oxide nanomaterials. Throughout delicately managing the calcination requirements, including process temperature, atmosphere, and consuming time, the metal ion centers coordinated to organic linkers can be turned into porous metal oxide nanocomposites or nanostructures. A majority MOFs are synthesized just by hydrothermal method and the yield of many MOFs is very low. To address the present restrictions and reasonable explications, it should be noted that MOF-derived metal oxide nanostructures have exhibited high potential as electrode materials for supercapacitor applications; however, they currently experience some principal shortcomings. Concerning the MOF utilization in SCs and present problems, poor electrical conductivity at higher charge–discharge rates due to poor bulk electron conduction, leading to an overall resistance rising. Besides, short cycle life at higher rates due to the increase in resistance and inadequate Faradaic redox reactions at higher current densities as well as low electrode surface area, resulting in a small contact area between the electrolyte and the electrode material. Also, the limitations in the diffusion distance of the electrolyte inside the pores of the metal oxides due to the high crystallinity of MOF-derived metal oxides is another obstacle that researchers should sacrifice more attempts to explore the efficient strategies to take the barriers. To address the potential perspectives for enhancing the electrochemical performance of SCs, before heat treating that compounds in the air, the preheating MOF precursors under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere leads to a considerable increase in the surface area of MOF-derived metal oxides. This is advantageous for inhibiting the rapid release of destructive volatile gases (e.g., $\mathsf{N O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{\overbrace{}}$ ) which would have led the collapse of the frameworks. Furthermore, combining MOFderived metal oxides with highly conductive carbon substrates such as carbon nanotubes (CNTs), reduced graphene oxide (rGO), and carbon black, can increase the electrical conductivity. Moreover, through diminishing the crystallinity of the MOF-derived metal oxide compounds, the improvement in the distance can be taken in which electrolyte can diffuse inside the pores of the metal oxides. While it is assumed that electrolyte can diffuse for a restrained distance $({\sim}20~\mathrm{nm})$ inside the pores of crystalline oxide materials, amorphous oxide materials provide a larger diffuse distance $(\sim50~\mathrm{nm})$ for electrolytes. On the other hand, balancing the pore size of the MOF-derivatives with the size of the electrolyte ions via controlling of the heating conditions known as a prominent method to overcome the electrolyte ion diffusion shortcomings. As mentioned previously, the hybridization with carbonbased materials and functionalization of MOF-derived metal oxides through heteroatomic doping, and phase-controlled will support the realizing potential advantage of MOF-derived metal oxides for high-performance supercapacitors; whereas, there is plenty of room to introduce highly conductive MOF-derived metal oxides for supercapacitor applications. besides, 1-D and 2-D materials can provide easy access for ion intercalation and deintercalation that can be loaded in MOFs, and optimizing the heating conditions to make nanoporous composites, improve the electrochemical features including high energy density, higher charge storage capacity, and ultrahigh reversibility. Achieving this object can be e paved by the utilization of the 3-D materials that can offer numerous reaction sites in their 3-D networks. However, controlling the dimensionalities of the metal oxides has been rarely studied for the desired SC applications. Aforementioned clarifications signify the authority of MOF-derived materials and their future importance for energy storage devices. Apart from the mentioned strategies, MOFs and their compounds as the supercapacitor electrodes just are investigated in the labs with neglected regards of practical application in daily life. However, the results form patent analysis takes us out of this dark stalemate to good headlines for commercialization and industrialization of MOF-based materials as excellent supercapacitor electrodes [165,242–248].
The rapid growth of technology and growing demand on portable electronic devices has led to increasing need to development of flexible and wearable energy storage systems [250–253]. Nonetheless, application of MOF materials in portable and wearable technologies including smart clothes (e.g. intelligent bracelets), rollup display, electronic skins, wearable electronic devices for efficient energy storage (e.g. flexible lithium batteries and supercapasitors), and gas sensing devices is in its infancy. Because almost all MOF materials are produced in powder form, they are usually coupled with the fiber-shaped materials like single and multi-walled carbon nanotube (MWCNT) to find proper flexibility required in wearable devices. For instance, Huang et al [254] combined ZIF-67 and MIL-88 with MWCNT to fabricate a fiber-shaped, flexible and wearable device used for gas probing and energy storage. Their results showed that the MOF-derived fibers can be bent in different angles with remarkable performances for effective $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ detection and energy storage. Moreover, Huang and his co-workers [136] grew nanoflower-like of pseudocapacitive MnO inside MOF materials using a facile and convenient procedure to achieve threefold MOF- $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ as an efficient supercapacitor. The as-fabricated MOF$\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ combined with activated carbon to prepare electrodes for flexible supercapacitors. They concluded that their prepared flexible supercapacitor reveals outstanding performance in terms of capacitance and energy density among other supercapacitors. Yao and his co-workers [255] also developed a new strategy to generate wearable aqueous rechargeable batteries (ARBs) using MOF-based wearable fibers. To this purpose, they successfully fabricated a new MOF-derived NiZnCoP nanosheet on carbon nanotube fibers to achieve a flexible MOF with impressive energy storage property. Their results revealed that the wearable MOFderived NiZnCoP@CNTF delivers remarkable energy density of $30.61\mathrm{\mwh\cm^{-3}}$ and specific capacity of $0.092\mathrm{~mAh~cm}^{-2}$ when it is used as electrode material in ARBs. Furthermore, Wang et al [256] synthesized a flexible conductive porous electrode by combining ZIF-67 crystals with polyaniline (PANI) over a carbon cloth (CC). The flexible PANI-ZIF-67-CC exhibited extraordinary electrochemical performances with areal capacitance of $2146\mathrm{\mF\cm}^{-2}$ . Furthermore, they used two identical pieces of PANI-ZIF-67-CC to fabricate a flexible solid-sate supercapacitor with potential applications as portable energy storage devices and wearable electronics. In another work to synthesize flexible supercapacitors onto CC, a MOF-derived hollow $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ arrays was synthesized by Wang et al [257]. They concluded that $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ over CC shows remarkable electrochemical performance and long cycle life when it was employed as flexible electrode in supercapacitors.
## 3. Different component within MOFs
Many investigations have been extensively carried out to make a combination of MOF and other compounds to overcome the current drawbacks living in that porous molecules. In this section we are going to discuss the application of MOF derivatives and MOF composites used in supercapacitors regarding to specific capacitance as a prominent electrochemical option.
## 3.1. Metal and active materials used in MOFs
Up to now a long range of high efficient materials have been enthusiastically employed in supercapacitors [259–260] such as carbon based materials for example graphene [262–263], carbon [264], carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [265], carbon fiber [266] and carbon aerogel [267] as expanded as several metal hydroxides or oxides like $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ [268], $\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{5}$ [269], NiO [271–271], $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ [273–273], ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ [275–275], $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [276] and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ [277]. Meanwhile, other types of materials known as conducting polymers have been investigated to replace carbon based and metal oxides such as polypyrrole [278] and polyaniline (PANI) [99,279– 280]. In fact, MOFs made of assembling of metal ions and ligands by organic nature, which are naturally porous and crystalline, have experienced a great interest to use in supercapacitors and rechargeable batteries because of particular behavior of cation redox reactions and also make a good situation to electron pathway and furthermore linkers can improve the charge transfer [188,281–283]. Substantially, the nature of electrodes which differed by the carbon-based and non-carbonaceous materials, determine the efficiency of electrodes in energy storage systems. Additionally, MOFs with their structures and unique features provide the promising opportunity to consider how it possible to achieve outstanding electrochemical SC devices. To realize the highest performance procedure, despite the metal oxide and carbon-based electrodes, efficient design of MOFs, is a critical challenge. For instance, Liang Shao et al. [284] constructed $\mathsf{P A N I}/$ UiO66 as MOFs that made from grown molecular chain of polyaniline inside UiO-66 pores followed by boosted properties both electrochemistry and conductivity with specific capacitance by $1015\mathrm{F/g}$ at current density of $1\ A/\mathbf{g}$ [284]. Also, composite of Zn-MOFPANI had been constructed as supercapacitor with excellent performance. At a current density of $1\ \mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ was reported a specific capacitance of $477\mathrm{F/g}$ for these carbonized composite [285]. Based on revealed results Lee et al. worked on the cobalt-based MOFs in aqueous electrolyte, which exhibit an effective pseudo capacitive treatment when considered the capacitive behavior of Co-based metal organic frameworks to serve in supercapacitor device by using of an aqueous media [286]. The Co-MOFs were recently prepared in various solvents [287]. Capacity for Co-MOFs was reported $958.1\mathrm{F/g}$ . It was also shown that the solvent obtained from the mixture of ethanol and DMF is quite suitable for Co-MOF. Fig. 14 shows the mechanism of Co-MOFs formation in various solvents. Acquiring power density and energy density in outstanding values is rapidly developing to apply in electric devices. Basically, metal oxides are significant candidates to employ such as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [289– 290], $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ [292–292], $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ [294–295], and NiO [297–298]. Particularly ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ is cheap and rare metal oxide with good redox properties and environment-friendly has attracted many attentions while shows lower specific capacitance rather than other metal oxides such as $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ (560, $1169\mathrm{F/g}$ respectively). Enormous efforts have been carried out to investigate the pseudocapacitive features of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ [300–302] and Ce-BTC as a sacrificial template was synthesized by using of [Ce(1,3,5-BTC) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)6]$ $1,3,5{\mathrm{-BTC}}=1,3,5.$ -benzene tricarboxylate) and used as an exo-template due to its specific possessions to use in a supercapacitor. CV analysis and other measurements indicated that addition of a rapid redox active couple accompanied with closely matched potentials sufficiently affected capacitance properties of MOF-derived $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ with the significantly great specific capacitance of $1204\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ . Moreover, cycle stability of that was at an excellent value of 5000 charge/discharge cycles with almost $100\%$ capacity remaining [303]. A Zn-doped Ni-MOF has also been synthesized and employed as an effective electrode to use in a supercapacitor with the specific capacitance of 1620– $860\mathrm{F/g}$ was obtained at 0.25 and $10\ {\mathsf{A}}/{\mathsf{g}}$ . Also, retention capacity after 3000 cycles was reported $91\%$ of initial capacitance. Enlarged interlayer distance, layered structure and porous character are significant ingredients which increased both performance and electrochemical properties [304]. When Ni-based layered MOF employed as an electrode with desirable cycling stability and rate capability showed $10\%$ capacitance loss after 3000 cycles and exhibited a qualified specific capacitance around $1127\mathrm{F/g}$ attributed to layered structure and exposed facets [305]. The nanosheets based on Ni-MOF synthesized with hydrothermal method [306]. It was observed that by adjusting of trimesic acid and nickel ion, where molar ratio could be created a different hierarchical structure with high porosity [258] confirming an improvement of SC’s qualifications with specific capacitance of $1057\mathrm{F/g}$ $\textbf{(\textregistered~1~A/g)}$ (Fig. 15). Recently, it was shown that MOFs and GAs (graphene aerogels) are two very proper representatives for the supercapacitors with high properties. Then, Liu and colleagues [307] synthesized two compositions based on the Solvo-thermal procedure which obtained by grafting of the $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ion on the surface GO, in which the specific capacity, scan rate, and retention ratio were reported $353\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g},20\ \mathrm{A}/\mathrm{g}$ and $74.4\%$ respectively. Hence, the presence of halogen and hydroxyl ions in the final structure promoted the performance of supercapacitors [308]. Moreover, M. liu developed a graphene aerogel assisted method to breakbulk MOF (Cobased MOF, Co $(\mathrm{mIM})_{2}$ $\mathrm{\dot{\mIM}}=2$ -methylimidazole) crystals into monodisperse metal oxide hollow nanoparticles (NPs) (Fig. 16a). To preparation of $\mathsf{C o O x}/\mathsf{N G}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{A}$ and C/NG-A (Fig. 16b), by mixing graphene oxide (GO) dispersion with ammonia (chemical dopant) through a hydrothermal reaction $(160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $N$ -doped graphene hydrogel was initial synthesized. The residual solvent in the asprepared hydrogel was exchanged with fresh deionized water and methanol before Co-MOFs polyhedrons were in situ grown on $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped graphene sheets by immersing the hydrogel in MOF precursors. They found that the parent Co-MOF nanocrystals derived monodisperse cobalt oxide (CoOx) hollow NPs $(35\ \mathrm{nm})$ 1 were successfully dispersed in $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped graphene aerogels (NGA) and uniformly spread on graphene sheet surface after a simple thermal activation. The noted CoOx/NG-A worked as a new electrocatalyst with excellent activity for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in an alkaline solution. Besides, based on the Co removing from the $\mathsf{C o O x/N G{-}A},$ a nitrogen-rich 3-D carbon material (C/NG-A) was obtained that presented outstanding rate capability $(305~\mathrm{F/g}$ at $50~\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{g})$ , high specific capacitance $(421\mathrm{F/g}$ at a current density of $1\ \mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g})$ , and cycling performance $99.7\%$ capacitance retention within 20,000 cycles in an all-solid-state symmetric device when utilized as electrode material in supercapacitors.
## 3.2. MOFs and MOF-derived carbon-based materials
Different derivatives of metal organic frameworks make it possible to design really efficient porous electrode in SC systems. Three different types of metal organic frameworks known as MOF-5, Al-PCP and HKUST-1, were used as carbon source and template at high temperatures (1073 K) to form porous carbon. The originated carbon-based on HKUST-1 MOFs exhibited lower performance and electrochemical stability compared to Al-PCP and MOF-5 based carbon. Among these MOFs for Al-PCP based carbon showed better performance and specific capacity around $232.8\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ in aqueous electrolyte [310]. Also, MOFs are promising materials which can be employed in the synthesis procedure of different functional inorganic and organic materials to apply for a varied range of applications. Porous oxide nanostructure materials such as $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and $\mathtt{C u O}$ have synthesized through employing MOFs for different applications such as oil recovery, glucose evaluation, water purification, organic pollutants removing and supercapacitors in which these metal oxides have been investigated as anode materials for lithium (Li)-ion batteries [291,311–314]. Also, metal organic framework derived carbons have been widely studied to challenge different aspects of charge storage issues in organic electrolytes like ionic liquids and aqueous environments such as sulfuric acid and potassium hydroxide to consider the supercapacitor performance and develop its efficiency [316–318]. ZIF-8 as a Zn based zeolitic imidazolate framework pyrolyzed at a varied range of temperature to obtain specific capacitance of $214\mathrm{F/g}$ with supreme surface area $(1110\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{.}$ in aqueous bulk of sulfuric acid $(5~\mathrm{mV}~s^{-1}$ of scan rate) [319]. The eminent surface area of $2872\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and specific capacity $(232\mathrm{{F/g})}$ were reported by using of Nano-porous carbon synthesized through applying MOF-5 with 3-D channels as a template for polymerization of polyforfuryl alcohol and pyrolysis at high temperatures $(1000~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ [320] where MOF-5, ethylenediamine, and carbon tetrachloride exhibited more surface area $(2222\ m^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1};$ ) and capacitance reached to 271 and $156\mathrm{F/g}$ , while energy density obtained by 31.2 and 9.4 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ in nonaqueous and aqueous medium, respectively [321]. A worm-like mesoporous carbon was obtained by using of glycerol and a template (Zn-based metal organic coordination polymer) with a good pore volume (PV) $(3.1\bar{4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and specific area $(2587\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , while the capacitance value achieved as $344\mathrm{F/g}$ in an aqueous medium of KOH [322]. Therefore, symmetric configurations of MOF-derived carbonaceous was operated with poor values of energy density compared to electric and plug-in hybrid electric vehicles, 150 and 57–97 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ respectively [323]. Many ambitious attempts have been successfully accomplished to develop the energy densities of supercapacitors excepted from power density. Lithium-ion electrochemical storage and supercapacitors can mix to each other which known as Li-ion hybrid electrochemical capacitors, or Li-HECs that can be fabricated with carbonaceous and Li materials and use as a counter electrode. The insertion type of electrode can provide a high energy density with high surface area and providing high-power density. $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ with a spinel phase regarding to the eminent electrochemical properties attracted a tons of attempts towards providing higher energy density [325–331].
$\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ and MOF-derived carbon with high porosity and surface area were employed as a counter electrode and cathode in the organic medium. Zn-based metal organic framework or MOF5 was pyrolyzed to gain a notable carbon surface area with $2714\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}}^{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}$ which showed crumpled-sheet assembled porous morphology. The behavior of supercapacitor was considered by single electrode configuration versus lithium indicated a significant cyclability (with $82\%$ and galvanostatic cycles after 10,000 with the energy density of 20 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ where the specific capacitance achieved by $149\mathrm{F/g}$ . By applying $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ as anode and MOFderived carbon as a cathode in the Li-HEC, (MOF-DC based Li-HEC) indicated superior power capability and showed nearly 65 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ value for specific energy density which is significantly higher than traditionally available activated carbon with almost 36 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ . However, more developments are required to achieve excellent energy density to construct favorable electrochemical storage means. [332]. B. Lin et al. [333] reported a mesoporous $\mathsf{L a F e O}_{3}$ perovskite derived from MOF gel for all-solid-state symmetric SCs. They synthesized perovskite-type oxides through the Sol-gel method due to providing excellent chemical characteristics and texture of particles, superior chemical homogeneity, and possibility in controlling the size and morphology. However, their intricate, time-consuming process and limited porosity adjusting, prevented their potential application in the development of energy storage materials. MOF gel (MIL-100-Fe) used for fabricating mesoporous perovskite-type materials as schematically shown in Fig. 17. The MOF gel can be synthesized in controllable time duration without a multi complexing agent, particular pH conditions, and high-temperature treatment. The synthesized perovskite $\mathrm{LaFeO}_{3}$ with mesoporous structure and the pore size of $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ contribute to a specific capacitance of $241.3\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ at $1\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ with retention of $68\%$ even at a high current density of $20\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ . Meanwhile, the allsolid-state symmetric supercapacitor exhibited a wide potential window of $1.8\mathrm{V}$ , and an outstanding energy density of 34 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of 900 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ with $92.2\%$ capacitance retention after 5000 cycles, outperforming perovskite-type electrode materials, and even comparable to MOF- derived iron oxides. Thus, developing perovskite-type electrodes exposes a new direction to assemble porous perovskite-type electrode materials for supercapacitors, combining the chemical homogeneity of sol–gel technology and the porous properties of the MOF template.
Nano-porous carbon was provided by ZIF-8 in the absence of carbon sources with narrow pore size and a distinguished area which exhibited high stability and electrochemical capacitance with highest values compared to other carbon-based supercapacitors [319]. The wettability of porous carbon materials and electrical conductivity can be outstandingly improved by nitrogen doping due to channeling the high specific capacitance through a pseudocapacitive effect. Totally, MOFs-derived porous carbon (MPC) materials by providing remarkable electrical conductivity, exceptional morphology and higher surface area known as promising next-generation materials for practical applications of supercapacitors. Specifically, due to achieving superior conductivity of heteroatoms doped microporous carbon including nitrogen (N), phosphorous (P), and Bohr (B) atoms, they can effectively enhance electrochemical properties of SC devices. Therefore, MPCs and heteroatoms doped composites considered as high potential and desiring candidates for high rate performance and long cycle life SCs. It is clear that studies should be carried out for such materials which improves their wettability and interaction with aqueous electrolytes. Thereby, launching heteroatoms (P,B,N) or hydrophilic groups (–OH, –NH) with active sites into the frameworks to improve the wettability can accelerate ion transfer in bulk [183]. Accordingly, an asymmetric supercapacitors based on carbon nanotubes@nickel oxide nanosheets $(\mathsf{C N T@N i O})$ core–shell composites as positive electrode and porous carbon polyhedrons (PCPs) as negative electrode in aqueous KOH solution as electrolyte. The CNT@NiO core–shell hybrids were prepared through a facile chemical bath deposition method followed by thermal annealing, while PCPs were obtained by direct carbonization of Zn based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). Owing to their unique microstructures, outstanding electrochemical properties have been achieved in three-electrode configuration, (e.g., 996 $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ at $1\ \mathrm{A/g}$ , for the CNT@NiO electrode within $_{0-0.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ window, and $245~\mathrm{F/g}$ at $1\ \mathrm{A/g}$ for the PCPs electrode within $-1{-}0\vee$ window. Fig. 18 shows the electrochemical performance of pure NiO and $\mathsf{C N T@N i O}$ composites that obtained by ZIF-8 polyhedrons [334].
Regarding to the application and current development of metal organic frameworks and their composites are derived as prospective and outstanding electrode materials due to large pore volume and accessible metal sites, flexible structure, well-regulated morphology, and expressively high specific surface area, for next energy storage supercapacitors based on the reported experimental results. In spite of mentioned promising advantages of MOFs, some crucial challenges are still in pending contributing to the practical utilization of both pure MOFs and their derivatives [43,335–338]. Regardless to the environment, the porous frameworks must be further developed in lights of air-stability, electrical conductivity, tunability, affordable, and feasible market availability are contributing an emerging role in applicable SCs. Among them electrical conductivity is a main factor which typically can be boosted by accretion of frequent types and morphology of carbon materials where MOFs suffering from low efficiencies. Therefore, exceptional morphology, reaction chemistry, the derivation methods, and structure must be extensively investigated to introduce scalable manufacturing, cost-effective, and hopped-up electrode materials for fierce tendency ultracapacitors [198,339–342].
Nano-porous carbons (NPCs) have been prepared straight through carbonization of Zinc based metal organic frameworks (ZIF-8) to employ in biomedical and sensing to consider their performance [320,343–344]. And then a new symmetric supercapacitor has been designed based on this method which exhibited a great surface area nearly $(1523\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}).$ . The pore size was almost $1\mathrm{nm}$ for NPC particles with a high potential to apply as a capacitive electrode in the supercapacitor. Based on results at $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ rate of scan, the specific capacitance was reported as $251\mathrm{F/g}$ . And also, the specific energy and specific power energy were obtained $10.86\mathrm{Wh}$ $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ and 225 W $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ respectively. These results exhibited that ZIF8 derived nano-porous carbons require more developments to reach better results particularly in cycle life and rate capability by optimizing of NPC particles and pore structures for next generation of supercapacitors [345]. However, the handling of ultracapacitors has already serious contend, majoring with low energy density, limiting their utilization in numerous terms, specifically in high-demanded energy capacity cases. To enhance the energy density, a large number of scientists has intensively worked through two basic approaches including: developing high capacitance electrode materials, as well as heightening wide potential windows electrolyte materials [337,346].
Based on the substantial utilization of such materials to SCs, MOFs with sizable pore textures, high surface area, and high carbon contents have been increasingly used as sacrificial templates for preparing nano-porous carbon (NPC) materials [321,347]. Yamauchi et al developed EDLC systems by introducing nanoporous carbon electrode materials which prepared through the carbonization of ZIF-8 [349–349]. With more profound elemental comprehension through both experimental and theoretical approaches, many investigations leading towards employing pristine MOFs as high potential electrode as they applied more than just significant precursors for nano-porous carbon (NPCs) materials. Ni-MOF $(\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2})$ as an electrode material was used to explore the feasibility of applying pure metal organic frameworks to SCs without furthering binder or conductive supporting materials in a two-electrode device and represented high specific capacitance of $111~\mathrm{F/g}\left(0.05~\mathrm{A/g}\right)$ [350]. Recently, studies related to MOF applications for electrode materials are no longer being limited to pristine/porous MOFs and designing with new synthesis strategies contributed to fabrication of high-efficient SCs have become more diverse and in-depth. For the first time, a new material that called $\mathrm{H-}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{CoNC}$ , $\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}@Z n C o_{2}O_{4}}$ and $Z n C o_{2}O_{4}@Z n-C o-S.$ was designed and synthesized on Flexible CNTFs by hydrothermal method [351]. The steps of its construction and the results of this research due to the singular and porous framework of hierarchically, have increased the penetration and reaction of ions in the cavities. Nano-porous carbons (NPCs) and nano-particle of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ were obtained through zeolitic-imidazolate framework (ZIF-67), based on perfect control on annealing condition. ZIF-derived nano-porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ compared with ZIF-derived carbon exhibited 148 and $350\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ surface area. And also, they preserved as promising electrode while the specific capacitance achieved as 508 and $250\mathrm{F/g}$ for nano-porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and nano-porous carbon, respectively. To inspection the proficiency of ZIF-derived nanoporous materials were employed in two different type of supercapacitor as asymmetric and symmetric (ASC and SSC respectively) [352]. As another highly potential porous framework, ZIF-67 which contains cobalt elements and $N\mathrm{.}$ -containing organic ligands can be used to form $N$ -doped nanoporous carbon (negative electrode) and nanoporous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (positive electrode) in a symmetric supercapacitors (SSCs). Additionally, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ materials are promising electrodes by owing superb electrochemical stability with high theoretical capacitance $(3560~\mathrm{F/g)}$ along with being affordable and environment compatibility. Therefore, electrodes derived from ZIF-67 are considerable materials for SCs [354–354]. Accordingly, nanoporous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and NPCs derived from annealing ZIF-67 in air and nitrogen atmosphere respectively, based on various thermal treatment procedures reported in Yamauchi et al. [352]. Both prepared materials were employed in asymmetric and symmetric SC while they preserved the original polyhedral morphology of ZIF67. By fabricating the negative and positive electrodes, the SSC system showed low specific energy densities $(\mathsf{C o}_{3}0_{4}//\mathsf{C o}_{3}0_{4}$ ${\sim}8\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}$ , carbon//carbon $\sim7~\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}.$ ). While the ASC system using the negative (NPCs) and positive $(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4})$ electrodes, combined the superiority of NPCs including good stability, high conductivity, and large surface area to both high surface area with the pseudocapacitive $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and exhibited an admirable energy density of 36 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ . Through the broad-mindedness of both acquired experimental results with theoretical calculations on the correlation between material structure/composition and electrode performance, a stable gradient and multi-level porous structure could be made, which should be profitable to boost the ion transmission channel, therewith raising the rate performance. Fig. 19 illustrates an ASC cell consisting of NPC and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and CV and galvanostatic curves in which NPC has a significant role in charge delivery and rectangular shape of CV curves indicating the acceptable capacitive features and good reversibility [352].
The performance of porous carbon depends substantially on the surface area and pore distribution [356–359]. In order to control the pore structure of porous carbon materials to employ in supercapacitor distinct strategies have been developed that chemical and physical activation [361–361] are best known methods with other ones such as template carbonization [363–365] and carbide-derived carbon [321,366–367]. Porous carbons can produce in different ways, for example these materials can be provided by employing metal organic frameworks as a good template and furfuryl alcohol $(\mathsf{C}_{5}\mathsf{H}_{6}0_{2})$ as a coherent of carbon [369–369]. One of the well-known MOFs is MOF-5 termed as $\mathrm{(Zn_{4}O(O O C C_{6}H_{4}C O O)_{3})}$ with a 3-D channel which can be prepared at room temperature [322,370–371]. Hu et al. [321] reported a different type of carbon MC (MOF and carbon), PMC (phenolic resin and carbon) and MAC (MOF, carbon and additional carbon source) with a range of pore structures. And also, tetrachloride and ethylenediamine were applied as a carbon source in this investigation and then activated to obtain enhanced tune pores and improved texture in the presence of KOH. Organic and aqueous media have been used to consider the performance and relative parameters. Results showed that activated MAC is a promising material to employ in supercapacitor with the high surface area $(2222\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ). Energy density in $6\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH was reported of 31.2 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ with capacitance of $271\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ in both environments. Based on volumetric calculations activate MAC exhibited the highest power density of $2.4\mathrm{\kW/L}$ in aqueous media during charging $(@10~s)$ while during discharging process of for activated PMC in organic media, the highest power density was obtained $5\ \mathrm{kW/L}$ (at 10 s) [372]. By using pyrolysis Cu-based metal, the organic framework was employed to produce $\mathtt{C u/C u}$ oxide fabricated graphitic carbon with nanostructures at $700-800~^{\circ}C$ to apply as an efficient electrode material for ultracaps with good performance $(700\mathrm{F/g}\ @2\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1})$ in which CV tests indicated high charge storing with almost $40\%$ of initial capacitance retention at low scan rate. Actually, these great results depend substantially on the suitable combination of functional groups with micro/mesoporous in the carbon media. By investigation of current–potential (i-E) responses, it was argued that the electrode materials obey the same procedure on the positive sweeps of all voltammograms by altering the upper limit potential with good electrochemical reversibility of the electrode materials [373]. while Cu MOF [374], Zn MOF [286] and Co MOF suffering from an instability in KOH (aq) [376–376], a well- designed carbon based composites which has enormously been exploited as a prominent doublelayer supercapacitor, were introduced for achieving advanced capacitors like reduced graphene oxide (rGO), with large surface area and high conductivity, exhibited extraordinary outcome related to electrochemical efficiency. As mentioned before, MOFs are inherently insulator that prevents power transmission, thereby, compositing of MOFs with nano-scale materials such as graphene is one of the promising procedures to improve the feature of MOFs materials [377]. In the meantime, rGO is due to outstanding properties such as improved conductivity and high surface area, is a distinguished candidate for electrode modification [163,378–379]. Choi et al. [376] investigated applications of graphene MOF composites to storage energy with $5.09\mathrm{~mF~cm}^{-2}$ by employing Zr and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate MOF/graphene. Designing and introducing of new MOFs to produce better and high performance is a typical method while tailoring MOFs and fabricating of metals into them is dramatically centralized to rapid and facilitated electron transfer with GO and rGO nanosheets. Doping Ni into MOFs with rGO was investigated by Banerjee et al. to study the mechanism and performance when MOFs show weak instability accompanied with the electrochemical reply because of the irreversible reaction of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ in large number of aqueous medias. As a practical method $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ can substitute with Ni to solve $Z\mathrm{r}$ problems as rGO serving, lead to generate enhanced charge transfer. Based on the Hummer method, graphene oxide was employed as a good precursor of rGO which obtained from graphite powder. According to Banerjee and et al. [380] GO ( $\cdot100~\mathrm{mg})$ was fabricated into the bottom of a flask and then water $\mathrm{\nabla_{100ml}}$ was introduced to obtain an inhomogeneous brown dispersion followed by ultrasonic bath (1 h). Then, hydrazine hydrate $\langle1~\mathrm{ml}\rangle$ was added and heated (100 ${\^\circ}\mathrm{C},24\ \mathrm{h}{\cdot}$ ) so that a black precipitate was obtained then filtered and collected. And also, MOF-rGO was synthesized by employing of various amounts of rGO in the chlorobenzene and DMF, and then $50\%$ of rGO was added to DMF $(40~\mathrm{ml})$ to be sonicated. After centrifugation $(3000\ \mathrm{rpm},5\ \mathrm{h})$ chlorobenzene was added $(5~\mathrm{ml})$ with constant stirring. Next, $Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $_{(0.6\ \mathrm{g})}$ were added with same amounts, and BDC $(0.334~\mathrm{g})$ was added to this mixture and dissolved in rGO/chlorobenzene/DMF. Then $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and TEA were dropped into the blend and then collected and washed to obtain MOF- $.5_{\mathrm{Ni}~50\%}$ rGO. Fig. 20 shows an illustration of Ni doping and ${\bf M O F}{\bf-}5_{\mathrm{Ni}}$ and varied the percentage of rGo [380].
The crystal structure of MOF-5 was changed by introducing of Ni, where CV results showed an increased capacitance without redox sites enhancing. Through loading of rGO, charge storage performance was promisingly developed in case of employing $50\%$ of rGO to $\mathsf{M O F}{-}5_{\mathsf{N i}50\%}$ in which the maximum capacitance was acquired as $758\mathrm{F/g}$ with forming an interconnected membrane like formation. Fig. 21 shows specific capacitance of Ni doping and rGO concentration in MOF- $.5_{\mathrm{Ni50\%}}$ [380].
Employing of conductive phases into rGO structure showed dramatic diminishing in charge transfer resistance, and showed lower ESR with higher power delivery and strong capacitance raising. This composite exhibited power and energy densities of 226.7 W $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ and 37.8 Wh $\mathrm{Kg}^{-1}$ , respectively with long-lasting stability. Also, it turns out the prepared composite possesses approximately-three times larger capacitance compared to double layer capacitance of rGO and Ni-doped MOF-5. Srimuk et al. [378] studied on a mesoporous composite materials based on rGO and HKUST-1 (Hong Kong university technology-1) metal organic frameworks to serve as a striking electrode material for supercapacitor applications in which a solid capacitor based on mesoporous rGO/HKUST-1 composites embedded on flexible CFP (carbon fiber paper) with an electrically graphitic sheet of short carbon fiber with a random arrangement. $10~{\mathrm{wt}}\%{\mathrm{~rGO/HKUST}}{-1}$ was employed as an effective composite to use as an electrode in ulrtacap. BET results showed specific PV and surface area of $0.78~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $1241~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which embedded on
CFP by using of spray-coating method demonstrated good capacity by $385\mathrm{F/g}$ $(@1~\mathsf{A/g})$ , with retention capacity of $98\%$ (2000 cycles) where specific capacity and potential window of $\boldsymbol{193\mathrm{F/g}}$ and $1.6\:\mathrm{V}$ being five times more than agglomerated reduced graphene oxide materials as an eminent electrode in supercapacitors. Furthermore, the functions of specific power and energy at 3100 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ and $42\mathrm{~Wh~kg^{-1}}$ respectively, ensures that this composite is a propitious material to employ in flexible supercap’s electrodes particularly [378]. Wu et al. [381] investigated Ni nano-particles fabricated in moderately graphitic carbon porous (GPC), embedded by carbonization of a Ni-organic framework (NiOF) and a solid state calcination was carried out on Ni-BDC (Ni- 1,4-bezene dicarboxylic acid) then transformed to amorph structure of carbon with fabricated NiO (Nickle oxide) nanoparticles at $400^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ temperature in air (atmosphere) condition while in $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and the presence of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, Ni nano-particles and carbon porous with partially GPC form was observed which supposed that GPC can enhance the conductivity. In fact, by applying higher temperatures, graphitization degree of carbon will increase simultaneously, and electrical conductivity boosts with a reduction of surface area due to crystal size rising. Here temperature of $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ is an optimum point to obtain high conductivity and surface area. CV results exhibited that nano-particles of Ni/NiO introduced on the porous carbon play redox center role to save charges. Of course, graphitic porous carbon (GPC) having macro-pores that provide tunable channels improving the electron movements. Ni-BDC showed a superior life cycle stability and specific capacitance of $30~\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g}$ during high discharge current attributed to structure properties [381]. Fig. 22 shows an illustration of Ni nanoparticles inserted in partially GPC to employ in supercapacitor application.
## 3.3. Mixed metal MOF in supercapacitors
Electrical double layer capacitors also are known as EDLC present large surface area like many carbon-based materials such as nanotubes, aerogel, and nanofoams which gather the energy. This energy storing comes out by making a double layer right between electrolyte (such as an ionic liquid or aqueous) and electrode through a well-known mechanism called as electrostatic interaction [383–386]. Pseudo capacitors depend on surface redox procedure on the electrode to produce capacitance [18,387–389]. $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ are introduced as a great candidate particularly to apply in supercapacitors, and spinel Zn-cobaltite are promising design due to more rich redox reactions and high ion conductivity to employ in supercapacitors [390]. JUC-155 is a mixed metal MOFs with 3-D structure $(\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O(B T C)_{2}(D M F){\cdot}H_{2}O)}$ was synthesized on the current solvent thermal procedure which can convert to $Z_{\mathrm{{nCo_{2}O_{4}}}}$ nano-porous as an electrode to use in a supercapacitor. By application of a mixed-metal MOF (MMOF) as a unique precursor for generation of oxides of mixed-metals, analysis resulted in $450\mathrm{F/g}$ specific capacitance when low rate scan is used and also by 1500 cycles, reliability is approximately $97.5\%$ of beginning capacitance. It is clear that by changing and replacing suitable MOFs better results will obtain. Kazemi et al. [391] introduced a mixed-MOF) (Co- and Mn-based MOF) which prepared through a simple onepot solvothermal method and employed as the electrode material for the SC. Notably, a Co–Mn MOF electrode showed a large surface area and excellent cycling stability (over $95\%$ capacitance retention within 1500 cycles). Besides, the exceptional areal capacitance of $1.318\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of Co–Mn MOF electrode in the KOH electrolyte exhibited an excellent pseudocapacitive behavior. Moreover, an asymmetric supercapacitor was assembled using Co–Mn MOF and activated carbon electrode as positive and negative electrodes, respectively. The fabricated supercapacitor showed a specific capacitance of $106.7\mathrm{F/g}$ at a scan rate of $10\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ and delivered a maximum energy density of 30 W h $\displaystyle{\log^{-1}}$ at 2285.7 W $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Fig. 23 shows comparative CV curves, charge–discharge diagram, Nyquist plot, and cycle life stability of the Co–Mn-based MOF (MOF-2) electrode [390]. The hierarchical nanosheets of Ni-Co sulfide on nickel foam constructed by Kai Tao et al. [392] as reliable (positive) electrode for employing in SCs. Newly synthesized metalized-MOF showed $1406.9\mathrm{F/g}$ (at $0.5\ \mathsf{A}/\mathsf{g})$ , as an excellent specific capacitance and remained by $88.6\%$ in 1000C/D life. Also, the value for energy and high-power densities reported as high as $24.8\mathrm{\:Wh\:kg^{-1}}$ and $849.5~\mathrm{W}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , respectively. In the electrodes of NiO based, the actual specific capacity is well below the special theory capacity of $2584\mathrm{F/g}$ [393]. Recently, Hou and his colleagues reported a new porous MOFs material for the use of NiO to use supercapacitors, which showed excellent performance in specific capacitance after 3000 cycles [394].
## 3.4. Micro and meso MOFs and their applications
Due to a narrow pore size of microporous MOFs, their application has been restricted because these materials are not able to accommodate larger guest parts as well as they cannot anchor to molecular functions. Many efforts should be devoted toward industrialize lab developments of micro-mesoporous MOFs (Fig. 24) through optimizing pore size and tunability design. Mesoporous MOFs have the pore size between 2 and $50\mathrm{nm}$ while microporous MOFs have pore size less than $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ in which micro MOFs hinder rapid molecular diffusion and mass transfer [139,395– 396]. Preparation and designing of meso MOFs are actually a difficult procedure since the co-ordination frameworks known as an
APT(Automatic programmable tool) for interpenetration or may disintegrate when the pores become augmented [397]. Moreover, the large extended pores which are substantially supported by flexible co-ordination connecting, consequently lead to diminish the physico-chemical stability when subjected to external inspiration, such as water, heat and etc., as it is normally-one of the significant challenges for boosting MOF materials [398]. Although trivalent cations such as $A|^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ and $\ln^{3+}$ and divalent cations such as $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ can develop chemical stability. Meanwhile $\ln^{3+}$ is a promising candidate to apply in MOFs because it shows excellent stability in water with low toxicity [96,399–403]. In a typical investigation rely on rigid backbone $\dot{4},\dot{4}^{/},4^{//}\mathrm{~-~}[$ benzene 1, 3, 5 - triyl - tris (oxy)] tri benzoic acid (H3BTTB) ligand, indicated good flexibility coordination the ether O (oxygen groups). And also, In (III) meso MOF was produced with 1-Dimensional channels with high thermal and chemical stability where employed as an electrode. Mesoporous MOF-437 $\smash{\{([\mathrm{In\(BTTB)}_{2/3}}$ (OH)] $(\mathsf{N M F})_{5}(\mathsf{H}20)_{4})_{\mathrm{n}}\}$ was synthesized and then activated to consider the supercapacitor performance in addition of remarkable gas adsorption potential. MOF-437 frameworks are able to conduct as an eminent meso-MOF material mindful of outstanding physicochemical stability with a crystal structure, demonstrating similar framework to CNTs, where employed for SCs as electrode materials [404]. MOF-437 showed largest and excellent 1-D regular channels with higher stability which can use as a promising material to develop the cyclability and obtain higher specific capacitance with higher gas uptake which facilitated by increasing the activation temperature, but this high temperature brings harmful effects on the nobility of MOFs. Thereby, for obtaining the optimal porosity, other activation methods should have developed significantly with remarkable chemical stability. Furthermore, based on their crystal form, the tunable channel walls were hydrophobic which was confirmed by vapor adsorption analysis.
4. The role of MOF composites in SCs regarding specific capacitance, energy and power density
High power and energy density are two important issues that direct researches to achieve developed energy storage devices. Supercapacitors have more power density with longer cycling life compared with current batteries [407–408]. Many efforts have been carried out to obtain more efficient with higher power density by developing in additives and materials [410–412]. Appropriate materials in supercapacitors must contain several particular features such as tailored pore size, high electrical conductivity and also possess a great surface area to reach a good supercapacitor. Metal organic frameworks are a good candidate to employ in this type of supercapacitors [413]. As mentioned previously, MOFs have a great surface area and customizing ability which can enable those to apply in many applications [414] as many attempts were done to use those as an electrode. Li-ion batteries were the first candidates to apply MOFs with this application in energy storage batteries. However, it did not result in significant success because they indicted a poor electrical conductivity with steric hindrance to Li-ion insertion attributed to pore size issues [416–416]. Supercapacitive behavior of MOFs has designed and applied with Co-Zn metal compounds which increased the gas adsorption and also (Co8-MOF-5) reduced the band gap [418–418]. Cyclic voltammetry results showed that Co8-MOF-5 based electrode operates as an electric double layer capacitor while employing acetonitrile in the presence of carbon black. Also, galvanostatic charge/discharge analysis indicated that Co8-MOF-5 has good stability and introduce sufficient electrical conductivity. However, the electrical conductivity can be developed by replacing improved MOFs in their structures properties and changing the type of electrolytes [375]. Kang et al. used $\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}$ and Hbtc to synthesize the Ni-base metal organic framework with a zigzag structure for chains and 1-D [418]. Three-electrode system was employed as a current method to consider the electrochemical features. The CV curves showed a rectangular shape close to an ideal shape. Results based on two following reactions,
$$
\mathrm{Ni^{+2}\ +\ O H^{-}\left(a q\right)\leftrightarrow\ e^{-}\ +\ N i^{+3}\ (O H)_{a d}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{\DeltaNi^{+2}(O H)_{a d}\leftrightarrow e^{-\Delta}+\Delta N i^{+3}(O H)_{a d}}
$$
$\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ and reverse reactions respectively in the range of $0-$ $0.5\mathrm{~V~}$ during potential sweep exhibited anodic and cathodic peak where the capacitance operated based on pseudocapacitance with a high rate reversible faradaic reactions [419]. During $\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{D}$ process a decreased capacitance was obtained for supercapacitor (by $726\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{g}$ to $313.8~\mathrm{F/g})$ which related to inner active sites and hydroxyl ion $(\mathrm{OH^{-}})$ diffusion with efficient redox transitions [418]. The utilization of Ni-based MOF as a pseudo capacitive material in supercapacitor and other storage energy devices exhibited good electrochemical stability and long cycle life with a high specific capacitance which expected more uncovering of current to open new explorations about storing mechanism and providing reliable design to develop efficient electrochemical properties of MOFs. As a new design of flexible supercapacitor, a MOF-based supercapacitor was prepared by intertwining and employing ZIF-67, represented in Fig. 25.
To obtain the capacitance of $2146\mathrm{mFcm}^{-2}$ , carbon was covered by Co-MOF electrochemically through a polymerization of polyaniline (PANI) at $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ rate of scan. Actually, the originated channels by PANI facilitate the electron transporting between internal surface of tailored MOF and external circuit and capacitance is many more than values reported ones even carbon nano tubes, grapheme and metal oxides. Moreover, for the first time, a new flexible solid state supercapacitor was designed and operated by employing of MOF which open a new strategy to overcome low conductivity and design more flexible solid state SCs [420]. Results from CV analysis indicated that a synergistic influence happens on intertwining ZIF-67/PANI with beneficial of both the pseudocapacitance (ZIF-67, PANI) and EDLC process (internal surface areas) as hybrid-constructed electrode in which power density and the energy density were evaluated as 0.245 W $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and 0.0044 mWh $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and retention capacitance of $80\%$ (2000 cycling tests $\ @0.1~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2};$ [420]. Xu et al. [285] synthesized carbonized MOF-PANI composite as a promising electrode material to employ in SCs by using of Zinc-MOF which calcinated at $800^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ . During the polymerization process of aniline $(C_{6}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , the MOF-carbonized is eligible to employ as the framework to carbonized PANI/MOF composites with sandwich structure. The ZMP-0.04 which contains $0.04\ \mathrm{g}$ of metal organic framework, showed a specific capacitance of $477\ \mathrm{F/g}\left(1\ \mathrm{A/g}\right)$ and demonstrated a qualified electrical conductivity when the composite rocket up the ability of PANI electrochemical performing and storing electrolyte with a profound role of the structure of carbonized MOF. Our recent investigations in the application of Co-based MOF in supercapacitors, we employed ZIF-67/POAP composite as a promising 