## 5.10.3. The most employed electrolytes
Concerning of the vital importance of the electrolytes in energy storage systems, due to their effective role in passing electrons and ion diffusion, the employed electrolytes were categorized in two main types including solid state, and aqueous electrolytes (inorganic and organic). Interestingly, inorganic electrolytes have been widely used where an affordable and efficient electrolyte was required. As it is expected from the charge transfer by electrolyte, ionic liquids as an organic electrolyte providing high conductivity, non-flammability, and low vapor pressure are a promising candidate with much improved electrochemical results. However, inorganic electrolytes such as KOH and ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ were highly used among other electrolytes due to ease access and cost-effectiveness. In addition, Gel electrolytes by providing good ionic conductivity, structing flexible device contributed to the charge mobility and charge carrier concentration, are breathtaking electrolytes. Table 7 summarizes a list of the electrolytes employed in supercapacitors when combined with MOF materials. Furthermore, the additives materials contributed to the ionic conductivity are examined and listed in the following table. Considering the patents filed on the application of ionic liquids in the supercapacitor and its potentials, it seems that the recruitment of ionic liquids (5 patents) with different structures, higher performance, and balanced expenses could be an option to improve the simultaneous use of ionic liquids with outstanding properties of MOFs.
## 5.10.4. MOF types as electrodes in SCs
In this section, we take a deep look at the application of different MOFs in the supercapacitor, concerning of the registered patents. Based on the reviewing the revealed patents over the used MOFs in SCs, it is supposed that the types of application of MOFs as an electrode can be categorized in the following parts:
Pure MOFs with an improved conductivity and surface area   
MOF with conductive polymers (specifically Polyaniline (PANI)) and ionic liquids   
MOF with carbon-based materials including CNTs, carbon cloth (CC), nitrogen-doped activated carbon, graphene and (functional) graphene oxide   
 MOF with nanomaterials   
MOF-layered structures (ZIF-67 $@$ ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 $@$ ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF67)   
Binary MOFs and mixed metal MOFs (Co-Ni, Co-Fe, Co-Mn, NiMn, Ni-Cu, Zn-Zr, Fe-Zr, Ni-Zr, Co-Zr, Mn-Zr, Zn-Co, Fe-Co, MnCo)   
 MOF as precursor (extensively used)   
Composited MOFs including: CC@ZIF-67/8-PPY (Carbon cloth $^+$ Polymer), MOF/CNT/Graphene, Template (ZIF-8/SBA15), PANI/ZnO@ZIF-8 on carbon cloth, Binary $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C o\ M O F+C N}$ $\mathsf{T}+$ Graphene, IL-Zr-MOF $@$ PANI (Ionic liquid $^+$ polymer), $\mathsf{N F}/\mathsf{\Lambda}$ GF/Zn-doped Ni-MOF (nickel/graphite foam; NF/GF), Zr-NDIMOF and Zr-NDI-BPD-MOF (N,N’-bis(terphenyl- $\cdot4,4^{\prime\prime}$ -dicar boxylic acid)naphthalenediimide (H4BTD-NDI)), Iron-cobalt bis metal oxide/nanofiber of PANI/foamed nickel sheet, ZIF-8 CuO $@$ $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ core–shell nano-arrays, and ZnO $@$ $Z\mathsf{n}_{3}$ $(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ core–shell nano-arrays.
Based on the above classified applications of MOFs in SCs, Table 8 presents a summary of almost all employed MOFs which have been considered at least once in the reported patents. Employing of the listed MOFs in SC devices make a sense of ease synthesis or access, affordability, desirable interconnected structure (for both precursor and composites), high surface area, remarkable conductivity and the electrochemical features discussed in Sections 3 and 4. The most marketable and widely used MOFs in supercapacitors are listed in Table 8 with their schematic structure among the 113 patents examined in this regard. As can be expected, based on the growth of published research and articles discussed in section 5, the listed MOFs have widely and repeatedly used in the field of supercapacitors. More broadly, there is a clear undercurrent of recruitment of other types of MOFs in this field. It seems that researchers and brainstormers should take a more specific look at using other types of MOFs providing more affirmative and desirable electrochemical and framework attribute.
## 6. Conclusion
The patent analysis contains useful information that can be identified by analyzing and examining technology. Authorities can handle programs in the related field of technology. This information enables large companies, start-ups, universities, research institutes, and investors to make smart decisions before spending time and investing in new technology. Also, the obtained data enables researchers to deeply understand the current state of a technology field and concern important details on the construction, development, and improvement of a product or technology. Supercapacitors as a promising energy storage device has enticed countless contemplation between researchers. High power density and long cycle life are characteristic features of supercapacitors. To overcome the inefficiencies and weaknesses of the electrochemical performance of SCs, different types of materials have been used as electrode materials. The electrochemical performance of electrodes and their structures is critical challenge dealing with SCs. Amongst different carbon-based materials and composites, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derivatives, have been increasingly employed in SCs. MOFs and their composites provide higher active sites and surface area. Different composites of MOFs, conductive polymers, can overcome some drawbacks of MOFs such as low conductivity. It is highly favorable to decrease the electron resistance. Co, Ni, Zr, Cu and other metal containing MOFs provided an excellent specific capacitance, higher power and energy densities. Actually, designing the perfect MOF in terms of conductivity, thermal and mechanical resistance, and tailoring is critical issue dealing with high electrochemical behavior. China with significant differences from the US and South Korea, holds the patent and intellectual property mark in this, and it is predicted that Chinese would overtake the developing MOF-based SCs from the other countries in near future. This review describes the high potential of MOFs in supercapacitors and we believe that new high conductive and perfect electrochemical performance composites of new designed MOF derivatives can revolute and enhance the supercapacitor performance. Among the long list of MOFs, UiO 66, UiO 67, ZIF-67, ZIF-8, ZIF-69, ZIF-95, ZIF-100, ZIF-5, MOF-5, MOF-2, MOF-74, MIL-53, MIL-101, MIL-100, MIL-77, $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}$ , $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ were repeatedly used. However, the current investigations need to be developed and extended through some following suggestions:
Developing higher surface area with more reliable organic ligands; we believed that commercialization of MOFs needs more sacrificing efforts on developing optimizing condition with designing structures owing larger surface/volume ratio or use efficient precursors to obtain more surface area. U sing more conductive structures with modifying mixed metal or conductive framework; to industrialize the utilization of MOFs, the unique properties of MOFs should be combined appreciatively with other conductive and reliable electrochemical materials. Mixed metal or bi-metal MOFs are a good option to increase the mentioned features. Combining high surface area with more conductive and synergic effect from designed compounds leads to more desirable results for SCs.
 Employing an extensive range of MOF with unique properties (Using new and different prepared MOF); based on our results from the current filed patents over SC, employing different new-designed MOFs with engineered morphology and structures can be directed to discover the real mechanism of the faradaic reaction and charge transfer on MOF-derivatives. Besides, there are plenty of rooms for designing MOFs, including promising metal (oxides) centers and linkers with tunability, porous, and adjusted pore size properties.
 Improving the electrolyte performance (mainly ionic liquids embedding). Conductive materials such as ionic liquids and conducting polymers (e.g., PANI) are well-known and widely used materials to increase specific capacitance and electron movements just in lab experiments. The inventors and assignees have preferred to use MOFs with the carbon-based substrates due to cost-effectiveness, facilitating the assembly process, flexibility, and structural advantages. At the same time, MOF-derived materials joined with that conductive option will propose similar or even higher electrochemical features. Facilitated electrolyte ion diffusion and more activated sites would increase the intercalation mechanism.
The path taken by the registered patents shows that there is a great desire to study on MOF-precursor, doping techniques, with conductive or metal oxide compounds to increase the electrochemical behavior. Our findings revealed that CNTs, graphene, nanomaterials, are highly talented combined options towards supercapacitors while developing more flexible, wearable, and more balanced-size electrolytes or simulations studies are necessary to explore the charge–discharge mechanisms of MOF-derived nanostructures with optimizing their electrochemistry for higher super capacitive display. Furthermore, concerning the commercialization of MOF-derived compounds, they must be examined wholly through robust charge–discharge analyses to meet the practical criterion of solid carbon electrodes. Consequently, meaningful investigation efforts should be offered for synthetic method modifications and to build their processability for business purposes.
Although many researchers have remarkably focused on developing promising supercapacitors and electrodes, we offer to develop solid-type and commercialize electrode preparation to get them as significant shares of the energy storage market. According to this registered patent review, it is clear that in order to occupy a large share of the market, the future research and investigations must direct toward the production of wearable devices to apply in smart-electronics and gadgets. There is still a long way to enable research to the industry and find its way into our daily applications. We encourage young scientists and researchers to focus their future research on the development of more active electrodes in wearable devices. This strategy will accommodate solving the current problems of the existing energy storage devices with the advancement of their technology and lead to substantial economic growth and turnover.
